(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the architecture of IPv4 Addressing. Detail Classful Addressing \(Classes A through E\), default subnet masks, and address ranges. Explain the transition to Classless Inter-Domain Routing \(CIDR\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structure of IPv4 Addresses:) Tj
T*
(   An IPv4 address is a 32-bit binary identifier uniquely assigning logical network identity to a) Tj
T*
(   network interface card.) Tj
T*
(   Represented in Dotted-Decimal Notation: four 8-bit octets separated by periods:) Tj
T*
(   Octet1.Octet2.Octet3.Octet4 \(e.g., 192.168.1.1\).) Tj
T*
(   Total theoretical address space: 2^32 = 4,294,967,296 distinct addresses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Classical Classful Addressing Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   Original RFC 791 partitioned the 32-bit address space into five fixed classes based on leading) Tj
T*
(   bits:) Tj
T*
(   - Class A \(Leading bits: 0\):) Tj
T*
(   * Range: 1.0.0.0 to 126.255.255.255. \(127.0.0.0/8 reserved for loopback\).) Tj
T*
(   * Allocation: 8 bits Network ID, 24 bits Host ID. Default mask: 255.0.0.0 \(/8\).) Tj
T*
(   * Capacity: 126 networks, each supporting 2^24 - 2 = 16,777,214 hosts. \(Allocated to massive) Tj
T*
(   entities like IBM, MIT, DoD\).) Tj
T*
(   - Class B \(Leading bits: 10\):) Tj
T*
(   * Range: 128.0.0.0 to 191.255.255.255.) Tj
T*
(   * Allocation: 16 bits Network ID, 16 bits Host ID. Default mask: 255.255.0.0 \(/16\).) Tj
T*
(   * Capacity: 16,384 networks, each supporting 2^16 - 2 = 65,534 hosts. \(Allocated to) Tj
T*
(   universities and large corporations\).) Tj
T*
(   - Class C \(Leading bits: 110\):) Tj
T*
(   * Range: 192.0.0.0 to 223.255.255.255.) Tj
T*
(   * Allocation: 24 bits Network ID, 8 bits Host ID. Default mask: 255.255.255.0 \(/24\).) Tj
T*
(   * Capacity: 2,097,152 networks, each supporting 2^8 - 2 = 254 hosts. \(Allocated to small) Tj
T*
(   businesses\).) Tj
T*
(   - Class D \(Leading bits: 1110\):) Tj
T*
(   * Range: 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255. Reserved exclusively for Multicast groups \(no) Tj
T*
(   host/network partition\).) Tj
T*
(   - Class E \(Leading bits: 1111\):) Tj
T*
(   * Range: 240.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255. Reserved for experimental research and future use.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Transition to Classless Inter-Domain Routing \(CIDR, RFC 1519\):) Tj
T*
(   - The Crisis: Massive address waste! An organization needing 500 hosts was forced to take a) Tj
T*
(   Class B block \(65,534 addresses\), wasting 65,000 IPs.) Tj
T*
(   By 1992, global routing tables were exploding and IPv4 addresses were rapidly depleting.) Tj
T*
(   - CIDR Solution: Abolished rigid class boundaries in 1993.) Tj
T*
(   Permits arbitrary prefix lengths designated by slash notation \(/n\), where 'n' represents the) Tj
T*
(   exact number of contiguous masking bits.) Tj
T*
(   Example: An organization needing 500 addresses is allocated a /23 block \(2^9 = 512 addresses\),) Tj
T*
(   eliminating 99% waste.) Tj
T*
(   CIDR enabled Route Aggregation \(Supernetting\), collapsing hundreds of route entries into single) Tj
T*
(   summary routes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
