45 780 Td
(   1. Mathematical Principles of Subnetting:) Tj
T*
(   Subnetting borrows bits from the Host portion of an IP address and assigns them to the Network) Tj
T*
(   portion,) Tj
T*
(   logically partitioning a single physical network into multiple smaller, independent broadcast) Tj
T*
(   domains.) Tj
T*
(   - Number of subnets created by borrowing 's' bits: Subnets = 2^s.) Tj
T*
(   - Number of usable hosts per subnet with 'h' remaining host bits: Usable Hosts = 2^h - 2.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Subnet Calculations for 192.168.1.0/24:) Tj
T*
(   - Base Network: 192.168.1.0/24 \(Default mask: 255.255.255.0; 8 host bits available\).) Tj
T*
(   - Required Subnets = 4 => 2^s = 4 => s = 2 borrowed bits.) Tj
T*
(   - Remaining Host Bits: h = 8 - 2 = 6 bits.) Tj
T*
(   - New Subnet Prefix: /24 + 2 = /26.) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet Mask in Binary: 11111111.11111111.11111111.11000000.) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet Mask in Decimal: 255.255.255.192.) Tj
T*
(   - Block Size \(Increment per subnet\): 2^h = 2^6 = 64 addresses per block.) Tj
T*
(   - Usable Hosts per Subnet: 2^6 - 2 = 64 - 2 = 62 usable hosts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Detailed Subnet Allocation Table:) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet 1:) Tj
T*
(   * Network ID: 192.168.1.0 /26) Tj
T*
(   * Usable Host IP Range: 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.62) Tj
T*
(   * Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.1.63) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet 2:) Tj
T*
(   * Network ID: 192.168.1.64 /26) Tj
T*
(   * Usable Host IP Range: 192.168.1.65 to 192.168.1.126) Tj
T*
(   * Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.1.127) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet 3:) Tj
T*
(   * Network ID: 192.168.1.128 /26) Tj
T*
(   * Usable Host IP Range: 192.168.1.129 to 192.168.1.190) Tj
T*
(   * Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.1.191) Tj
T*
(   - Subnet 4:) Tj
T*
(   * Network ID: 192.168.1.192 /26) Tj
T*
(   * Usable Host IP Range: 192.168.1.193 to 192.168.1.254) Tj
T*
(   * Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.1.255) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain IPv4 Packet Fragmentation. Detail the roles of the Identification, Flags \(DF, MF\), and Fragment Offset fields. An IP datagram with 4000 bytes total length \(20 bytes header + 3980 bytes payload\) passes through a link with MTU = 1500 bytes. Show the mathematical values for all fragments.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mechanism and Need for Fragmentation:) Tj
T*
(   Different physical link layers enforce different Maximum Transmission Unit \(MTU\) limits \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   Ethernet MTU = 1500 bytes; PPPoE MTU = 1492 bytes\).) Tj
T*
(   When a router receives an IP datagram whose total length exceeds the MTU of the next outgoing) Tj
T*
(   link, and the DF \(Don't Fragment\) bit is 0,) Tj
T*
(   the router must fragment the datagram payload into smaller chunks and transmit them as) Tj
T*
(   separate, independent IP datagrams.) Tj
T*
(   Reassembly occurs strictly at the FINAL destination host, avoiding router reassembly) Tj
T*
(   bottlenecks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Key Header Fields Governing Fragmentation:) Tj
T*
(   - Identification \(16 bits\): A unique integer assigned by the originating host; all fragments of) Tj
T*
(   the same original datagram share the IDENTICAL ID.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
