BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
45 780 Td
(   - Flags \(3 bits\):) Tj
T*
(   * Bit 0: Reserved \(must be 0\).) Tj
T*
(   * Bit 1 \(DF - Don't Fragment\): 0 = Fragmentation allowed; 1 = Do not fragment \(drop packet and) Tj
T*
(   return ICMP error if MTU exceeded\).) Tj
T*
(   * Bit 2 \(MF - More Fragments\): 1 = More fragments follow; 0 = Last fragment \(or unfragmented) Tj
T*
(   packet\).) Tj
T*
(   - Fragment Offset \(13 bits\): Indicates the relative starting position of this fragment's data) Tj
T*
(   payload within the original unfragmented payload,) Tj
T*
(   measured in units of 8-byte blocks \(64 bits\). Hence, payload sizes of all fragments except the) Tj
T*
(   last must be exact multiples of 8.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Numerical Fragmentation Problem Solution:) Tj
T*
(   - Original Packet: Total length = 4000 bytes \(Header = 20 bytes, Payload = 3980 bytes\). ID =) Tj
T*
(   54321, DF = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Outgoing Link MTU = 1500 bytes. Maximum payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes) Tj
T*
(   \(conveniently divisible by 8: 1480 / 8 = 185\).) Tj
T*
(   - Fragment 1:) Tj
T*
(   * Payload length: 1480 bytes \(bytes 0 to 1479\).) Tj
T*
(   * Total Length: 1480 + 20 = 1500 bytes.) Tj
T*
(   * ID = 54321; DF = 0; MF = 1 \(more fragments follow\).) Tj
T*
(   * Fragment Offset = 0 / 8 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Fragment 2:) Tj
T*
(   * Payload length: 1480 bytes \(bytes 1480 to 2959\).) Tj
T*
(   * Total Length: 1480 + 20 = 1500 bytes.) Tj
T*
(   * ID = 54321; DF = 0; MF = 1.) Tj
T*
(   * Fragment Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185.) Tj
T*
(   - Fragment 3 \(Final Fragment\):) Tj
T*
(   * Remaining Payload: 3980 - \(1480 + 1480\) = 3980 - 2960 = 1020 bytes \(bytes 2960 to 3979\).) Tj
T*
(   * Total Length: 1020 + 20 = 1040 bytes.) Tj
T*
(   * ID = 54321; DF = 0; MF = 0 \(Last fragment!\).) Tj
T*
(   * Fragment Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare IPv4 and IPv6 architectures. Detail the improvements introduced in IPv6 regarding: Address space, Header format simplification, Security, Quality of Service, Autoconfiguration, and Elimination of broadcasts.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Exhaustive Comparison Matrix:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Address Space Capacity:) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: 32 bits \(~4.3 * 10^9 addresses\). Depleted completely; relies on fragile NAT) Tj
T*
(   workarounds.) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: 128 bits \(~3.4 * 10^38 addresses = 340 undecillion addresses\). Provides millions of IP) Tj
T*
(   addresses per square meter of Earth's surface.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Base Header Format & Router Efficiency:) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: Variable header length \(20 to 60 bytes\) due to optional fields; includes IHL,) Tj
T*
(   Identification, Flags, Offset, and Header Checksum.) Tj
T*
(   Intermediate routers must recompute checksum at every hop.) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: Streamlined, fixed-length 40-byte base header. Checksum eliminated \(verified by Layer 2) Tj
T*
(   and Layer 4\); fragmentation removed from routers) Tj
T*
(   \(handled only by endpoints via Path MTU Discovery\). Optional parameters are chained cleanly via) Tj
T*
(   Extension Headers.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Address Autoconfiguration:) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: Requires manual static configuration or stateful DHCPv4 server infrastructure.) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: Built-in Stateless Address Autoconfiguration \(SLAAC, RFC 4862\) using ICMPv6 Router) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
