(   Advertisements and EUI-64 / random interface IDs.) Tj
T*
(   True 'plug-and-play' connectivity without dedicated DHCP servers.) Tj
T*
(   - 4. Elimination of Broadcasts:) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: Heavily dependent on disruptive broadcast traffic \(255.255.255.255, ARP requests\),) Tj
T*
(   which forces every host on the subnet to interrupt its CPU.) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: Zero broadcast addressing! All broadcast functions replaced by efficient Multicast) Tj
T*
(   groups \(e.g., Solicited-Node Multicast in NDP\) and Anycast.) Tj
T*
(   - 5. Security Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: IPsec was retrofitted as an optional add-on \(RFC 4301\).) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: Built from the ground up with native mandatory support for IPsec \(AH and ESP extension) Tj
T*
(   headers\).) Tj
T*
(   - 6. Quality of Service \(QoS\):) Tj
T*
(   * IPv4: 8-bit Type of Service \(ToS\) / DiffServ field.) Tj
T*
(   * IPv6: 8-bit Traffic Class field plus a dedicated 20-bit Flow Label field that enables) Tj
T*
(   ultra-fast hardware routing of real-time multimedia flows without deep packet inspection.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare Distance Vector Routing \(Bellman-Ford Algorithm\) with Link State Routing \(Dijkstra's Algorithm\). Detail their convergence speeds, routing metrics, protocol message overhead, and vulnerability to routing loops.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Distance Vector Routing \(e.g., RIP - Routing Information Protocol\):) Tj
T*
(   - Philosophy: 'Tell your neighbors about the entire world.') Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Each router periodically broadcasts its entire routing table \(destination,) Tj
T*
(   estimated distance vector\) to directly connected neighbors only.) Tj
T*
(   - Algorithm: Bellman-Ford equation: d_x\(y\) = min_v { c\(x,v\) + d_v\(y\) }.) Tj
T*
(   - Metric: Typically simplistic hop count \(RIP max 15 hops; 16 = infinity\).) Tj
T*
(   - Convergence Speed: Very slow; updates ripple slowly from neighbor to neighbor.) Tj
T*
(   - Routing Loops & Anomalies: Highly vulnerable to transient routing loops and the catastrophic) Tj
T*
(   Count-to-Infinity problem upon link failure.) Tj
T*
(   Requires heuristic countermeasures like Split Horizon, Poison Reverse, and Hold-Down Timers.) Tj
T*
(   - Scalability: Poor; strictly confined to small, non-critical networks \(< 15 hops\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Link State Routing \(e.g., OSPF, IS-IS\):) Tj
T*
(   - Philosophy: 'Tell the entire world about your immediate neighbors.') Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Each router discovers its directly connected neighbors and link costs, constructs) Tj
T*
(   a Link State Advertisement \(LSA\), and FLOODS it reliably to ALL routers in the area.) Tj
T*
(   - Topology Knowledge: Every router maintains an identical, complete Link State Database \(LSDB\)) Tj
T*
(   representing the global network graph.) Tj
T*
(   - Algorithm: Each router independently executes Dijkstra's Shortest Path First \(SPF\) algorithm) Tj
T*
(   on its local LSDB to compute the shortest-path tree with itself as the root.) Tj
T*
(   - Metric: Bandwidth-based cost \(Cost = Reference_Bandwidth / Link_Speed\).) Tj
T*
(   - Convergence Speed: Extremely fast; link state changes are flooded immediately upon failure;) Tj
T*
(   routers recalculate paths in milliseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Routing Loops: Completely immune to steady-state routing loops because every node calculates) Tj
T*
(   routes from a globally consistent topological map.) Tj
T*
(   - Resource Requirements: Higher CPU processing and memory consumption to store LSDB and execute) Tj
T*
(   Dijkstra's algorithm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
